# LIN-AGENT-RUNTIME-005: Adaptive Semantic Beam & Multi-Feature Hypothesis Ranking

## 1. Object & Scientific Mandate
Following `LIN-AGENT-RUNTIME-004` (which proved a 0% -> 100% Recovery jump via Beam Search at a cost of +18.6% tokens and +13.1% latency):
> **Can an Adaptive Multi-Feature Beam (combining topological distance, test assertion matching, contract invariants %, historical success, and on-demand candidate expansion) achieve the 100% recovery and localization gains of Beam Search while compressing prompt token consumption below Greedy levels?**

---

## 2. Multi-Feature Relevance Scoring Equation

$$\text{Score}(c) = 0.30 \cdot S_{\text{graph}} + 0.25 \cdot S_{\text{error\_match}} + 0.20 \cdot S_{\text{invariant\_match}} + 0.15 \cdot S_{\text{history}} + 0.10 \cdot S_{\text{effect}}$$

Where:
- $S_{\text{graph}}$: Topological proximity in DAG (callee vs entry vs leaf vs caller).
- $S_{\text{error\_match}}$: Lexical presence of candidate symbol/identifiers inside failing stack trace.
- $S_{\text{invariant\_match}}$: Density of attached `%` preconditions/postconditions relevant to the assertion.
- $S_{\text{history}}$: Historical success weighting across past repair sessions.
- $S_{\text{effect}}$: Pure vs IO effect safety signature `*(Pure)`.

---

## 3. Evaluated Arms
- **Arm C1:** `LIN Greedy` (Static single-node).
- **Arm C2:** `LIN Fixed Beam-3` (Full 3-candidate beam list in prompt).
- **Arm C3:** `LIN Fixed Beam-5` (Full 5-candidate beam list in prompt).
- **Arm C4:** `LIN Adaptive Multi-Feature Beam` (Surgical single-candidate dispatch per attempt with dynamic ranking).
